cartridge "TREE TUTOR FOR TOTS/BY JANET ARNOLD/1984"

' TREE TUTOR FOR TOTS
' A ColecoVision cartridge based on "Tree Tutor For Tots" by Janet Arnold,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette, March 1984 pp. 60-61 (VIC-20 and Commodore 64 BASIC;
' disk files tree tutor.vic and tree tutor.64; rules from the article and
' the listings in orig/). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game (an addition tutor for small children): an apple tree. The
' apples in the tree are the top number of the problem, the apples on the
' ground are the bottom number; the child adds them and answers. A right
' answer brings a bird swooping down to pluck an apple from the tree and
' drop it in the basket. A wrong answer says TRY AGAIN, with no penalty.
' Ten apples in the basket end the game: YOU GOT 10 APPLES IN n TRIES, and
' the bird lands on the message.
'
' From the original (orig/treetutor.64.bas, lines in brackets):
' - CHOOSE HIGHEST SUM (2-9) [190-200]; WHEN DO YOU WANT TO SEE FRUIT?
'   (1) ALWAYS (2) IF WRONG [210-240]; only keys in range are taken,
'   every other key is ignored [200, 240, 610].
' - top T = 1 .. F, bottom B = 0 .. F - T, so the sum never passes F; the
'   problem just answered right is never asked again at once [280-290].
' - an answer is a digit 1-9 [610]; every answer is a try [390]; a wrong
'   one shows the fruit for the rest of that problem [650: I = 0]; TRY
'   AGAIN with a low buzz [630-640]; HOORAY! and the count [660]; the
'   bird plucks an apple and the apple falls into the basket with a
'   falling tone [670-800]; after ten: YOU GOT 10 APPLE(S) IN X TRIES and
'   the bird flies down onto the message [810-900].
'
' Liberties:
' - Keypad digits answer (the natural input), and the stick and button
'   can pick a digit on the row of keys for a child without the keypad
'   habit. Settings are asked the same way on the title screen.
' - A right answer also shows the apples (with fruit option 2 the tree
'   may still be bare), so the bird has an apple to pluck and the child
'   sees the picture of the sum just found.
' - Ten apples heap up on the basket (the C64 stacked them in a column).
' - Ground apples are the same apples as in the tree (the article notes
'   the C64 drew two shapes; its author's son disliked that).
' - The end message waits for a button, then the title (the original's
'   HIT * TO PLAY AGAIN); a tries count up to 999 is shown.
' - New art, sounds and the title tune ("Apple Picking").
'
' Controls: either controller; keypad digits, or stick left/right and a
' side button on the row of keys.
'
' House rules: everything in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off and
' the display off; the title tune once, music first in the sound table;
' nothing flashes (the key pointer bobs at 2 Hz, the wings beat at 3 Hz).

